# lf colors. Two columns: a pattern, then an ANSI attribute string.
#
# Every value here stays inside ANSI 0-15 -- the 30-37 range, plus 01 for bold.
# That is the whole theme story for lf, and it is deliberate.
#
# foot resolves those sixteen numbers from its own palette, and
# quickshell/theme-switcher/Theme.qml rewrites that palette on every theme
# change. So a theme change reaches lf with no file of its own to generate --
# the glyphs are re-rendered by the terminal underneath them.
#
# Not live, though: foot has neither remote control nor a config reload, so it
# reads the new palette only at startup and a running lf keeps the colours it
# opened with. Reopen the window (SUPER+E) to see a theme change. This used to
# be live, when the terminal was kitty and Theme.qml pushed the palette over
# kitty's control socket; foot trades that for sixel, which is the swap
# lf/preview cares about.
#
# Use a 256-colour code here (38;5;N above 15) or a 24-bit one (38;2;R;G;B) and
# even that stops being true: the colour is then fixed in this file and lf
# becomes the one thing on screen that ignores the switcher entirely.
# foot/theme-colors.ini -- the file Theme.qml writes and foot.ini includes --
# exists because foot's own config could not be driven this way.
#
# What that buys costs something, and the cost is the palette is narrower than
# sixteen. Theme.qml builds all sixteen slots out of five accents:
#
#   1 and 9    accentRed
#   2 and 10   accentGreen
#   3 and 11   accentOrange
#   4, 5, 12 and 13  accentPrimary
#   6 and 14   accentCyan
#
# Two consequences, both verified against a live palette rather than assumed.
# Magenta is not a colour here: 35 is always exactly 34, so anything given 35
# is indistinguishable from a directory. And bold does not reach a brighter
# variant the way it does on a normal 16-colour terminal, because each bright
# slot holds the same value as its dim one -- 01 changes the weight and nothing
# else.
#
# So there are five hues to spend. Directories take accentPrimary and no file
# rule may use it. The other four each carry one category:
#
#   31 red      things that are archives or media -- opened by something else
#   32 green    things that run: executables and source
#   33 orange   things you read: documents and notes
#   36 cyan     things that configure: data and settings formats

# file types
di      01;34
tw      01;34
ow      01;34
st      01;34
ln      01;36
or      01;31
su      01;31
sg      01;31
ex      01;32
fi      00
pi      33
so      33
bd      33
cd      33

# archives and media
*.tar   31
*.tgz   31
*.gz    31
*.bz2   31
*.xz    31
*.zst   31
*.zip   31
*.rpm   31
*.deb   31
*.png   31
*.jpg   31
*.jpeg  31
*.gif   31
*.svg   31
*.webp  31
*.mp4   31
*.mkv   31
*.webm  31
*.mp3   31
*.flac  31
*.opus  31
*.wav   31

# source
*.sh    32
*.py    32
*.lua   32
*.qml   32
*.go    32
*.rs    32
*.c     32
*.h     32

# documents
*.md    33
*.txt   33
*.org   33
*.pdf   33

# data and configuration
*.json  36
*.yaml  36
*.yml   36
*.toml  36
*.ini   36
*.conf  36
